  "id": "Compound::DB01001 - Disease::DOID:3083",
  "verbalization": "Salbutamol, a member of the adrenergic beta-2 agonists class, is implicated in the treatment of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) through its ability to stimulate beta-2 receptors in airway smooth muscle, leading to bronchodilation. This mechanism is supported by the inclusion of Salbutamol in the adrenergic beta-2 agonists category and its association with Formoterol and Arformoterol, both of which are known to treat COPD. The shared lowest common ancestor between Salbutamol and these compounds, as well as their classification under beta-adrenergic agonists, suggests a common pharmacological pathway involving receptor activation and bronchodilation.\n\nThe evidence also highlights the potential for Salbutamol to contribute to COPD treatment through its role in the broader adrenergic system, which is involved in regulating airway function. However, the indirect nature of the relationship, mediated through the intermediate compounds, underscores the complexity of the pharmacological interactions involved. While the direct link between Salbutamol and COPD is not explicitly stated, the cumulative evidence from the paths suggests a plausible mechanism of action that aligns with the known therapeutic effects of beta-agonists in respiratory conditions.",
